export const debate = {"number":"256","id":"aron-ra-jones-christianity-dangerous-formal-rounds-2019","date":"2026-09-09","title":"Aron Ra vs Michael Jones — Is Christianity dangerous? (2019, formal rounds)","label":"Christianity and public harm","topicCategory":"religion-society-public-reason","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bQBFY1z_RvI","duration":"2 hr 34 min","motion":"Is Christianity dangerous?","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","sourceNote":"Public English captions were checked across the authorized formal debate rounds of this Bible and Beer Consortium recording from 2019. Selected passages were checked against the recording; material caption limitations are documented in the evidence record. Short quotations retain the locked caption wording; argument cards are faithful condensations. Only the opening statements and cross-examination (1:59–1:27:22) are assessed. All subsequent audience questions and answers, and both speakers' later closing statements, are excluded; neither speaker receives credit or penalties for that material. These scores are not an assessment of the complete recording.","scoringNote":"These AI-generated scores estimate argumentative performance in this transcript, not the truth of a worldview. They were computed exactly once from a frozen 28-move ledger after two isolated primary judgments and anonymous adjudication. Publication prose and subsequent fallacy and cognitive-bias review cannot alter the numerical result.","sides":{"pro":{"name":"Christianity poses serious dangers","speaker":"Aron Ra","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Christianity is not intrinsically dangerous","speaker":"Michael Jones","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":75,"con":82,"winner":"con"},"summary":"Aron documents scriptural and social harms, but Jones wins by repeatedly separating Christian identity from causation and challenging unadjusted comparisons.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"blasphemy is not a crime it's a right","context":"Aron invokes the Salem witch trials and the founders’ secular safeguards to contrast religiously governed law with legal protection for dissent and expression."},"con":{"text":"we have to do multivariate analysis of people in these countries","context":"Jones argues that national correlations require adjustment for competing influences before religion can be identified as the cause."}},"sections":[{"sectionId":"scripture-history-and-coercion","title":"Scripture, History, and Coercive Power","timebox":"2:23–1:04:44","score":{"pro":80,"con":81},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-religious-versus-political-violence","time":"2:23","role":"Supporting constructive","score":77,"words":"Aron distinguishes political imperialism from genuinely theological conflict, arguing that this causal filter still leaves Christianity with a substantial record of religious violence.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron does not simply count every death involving Christians, because his exclusion of British imperial conflict as political supplies a recognizable causal filter and improves the opening's intellectual fairness. Principal limitation: the Washington Times death comparison remains thinly documented, mixes an enormous historical category with an unspecified Muslim comparison, and never demonstrates which remaining episodes were produced by Christian teaching rather than nationalism, institutions, or opportunism. Live burden: To establish dangerousness, he still needs representative cases, denominators, and a defensible method for assigning causal responsibility when religious and political motives overlap. Locked score: The 77 rewards relevant framing, unusually explicit classification, and useful motion contact, while withholding stronger evidence-and-warrant credit because the quantitative headline and the move from selected history to Christianity generally remain substantially underdeveloped.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-old-covenant-not-prescriptive","time":"48:38","role":"Supporting reply","score":86,"words":"Jones argues that Christ fulfilled the old covenant, leaving Christians governed by commands of love rather than ancient Israel's coercive legal code.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones attacks the prescriptive bridge in Aron's scriptural case, explaining that ancient Israelite penalties do not become contemporary Christian commands and contrasting them with new-covenant duties of love. Principal limitation: His reply establishes an internal Christian distinction but does not address whether scripture's retention of divinely authorized violence can shape interpretation, political imagination, or claimed exceptions after the legal code becomes formally obsolete. Live burden: Jones must show not only doctrinal supersession but why recurrent Christian appeals to these passages are sufficiently aberrant that their foreseeable effects should not count against the tradition. Locked score: The 86 reflects excellent responsiveness, covenantal structure, and strong burden contact, moderated because the argument defeats present prescription more decisively than Aron's broader theory of scriptural danger.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-scriptural-violence-commands","time":"3:52","role":"Load-bearing constructive","score":81,"words":"Aron cites biblical commands to kill religious dissenters, destroy rival communities, enslave survivors, and execute alleged witches as a direct danger pathway.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron identifies concrete passages and concrete conduct—killing intimates for unbelief, destroying religiously different communities, enslaving survivors, and executing alleged witches—so the danger mechanism is far more specific than generalized moral condemnation. Principal limitation: He moves rapidly from commands within ancient legal and narrative settings to Christianity without explaining covenantal scope, interpretive authority, historical representativeness, or how present adherents are instructed to apply each passage. Live burden: The constructive needs a defended bridge from textual inclusion and claimed divine authority to contemporary Christian obligation, predictable behavior, or institutionally significant risk. Locked score: The 81 recognizes a forceful, source-linked, motion-central argument with clear inferential direction, while the reduced calibration and warrant components reflect overgeneralization across scripture, historical setting, denominations, and actual modern observance.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-misuse-does-not-establish-intrinsic-cause","time":"1:04:02","role":"Major direct reply","score":77,"words":"Jones concedes dangerous Christian episodes but argues that association and interpretive misuse do not show intrinsic religiosity or Christianity caused them.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones makes a valuable concession that dangerous conduct occurs while preserving the analytically separate question whether Christianity, intrinsic religiosity, or some independent political mechanism caused it. Principal limitation: His appeal to favorable studies is too compressed to answer Aron's historically specific coercion examples, and the word intrinsic risks narrowing the motion beyond its ordinary concern with whether a tradition can reliably generate serious danger. Live burden: He must distinguish opportunistic misuse from doctrinally enabled conduct with criteria that can classify cases, not merely demand correlation whenever scriptural interpretation and institutional power are the proposed mechanisms. Locked score: The 77 rewards clarity, relevance, and a legitimate causal objection, but responsiveness remains incomplete because conceding association without examining the cited sanctions leaves Aron's strongest responsibility argument materially alive.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-secular-law-protects-liberty","time":"7:34","role":"Load-bearing constructive","score":78,"words":"Aron contrasts witch trials and religious legal control with secular government, where supernatural accusations cannot determine criminal policy and blasphemy remains protected.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron connects religious authority to institutional mechanism, using the Salem trials and protection for blasphemy to show how excluding supernatural claims from law safeguards life, expression, and due process. Principal limitation: His claim that religious rule has always violated liberty is overbroad, the founding narrative is asserted but not demonstrated, and secular law's protective success does not isolate Christianity as the relevant cause. Live burden: He needs comparative institutional evidence showing that Christian premises predict coercive outcomes beyond dangers of concentrated authority, factionalism, and weak procedural protections. Locked score: The 78 reflects motion relevance and concrete protective contrast, while lower calibration and evidential scores register sweeping historical language, selective examples, and an incomplete inference from secular constitutional design to general Christian dangerousness.","tags":[{"label":"Base-rate neglect","type":"bias","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/base-rate-neglect/","context":"Aron claims religious rule always violates life and liberty, illustrating the claim with Salem and protected blasphemy."}]},"con":null},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-dissent-punished-as-capital-crime","time":"1:03:21","role":"Major direct reply","score":83,"words":"Aron answers the benign-trust account by distinguishing heresy, apostasy, and blasphemy, emphasizing Christian systems that made dissent damnable or capital.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron responds directly to Jones's benign account of faith by identifying distinct categories of dissent—alternative interpretation, abandonment, and rejection—and tying required belief to historically severe sanctions, including execution. Principal limitation: The historical and present-country claims receive little sourcing or denominational differentiation, and the evidence does not yet show when Christian commitments contribute to those penalties beyond their political appropriation. Live burden: Aron should demonstrate how authoritative doctrines, institutions, or recurring interpretive patterns connect salvation-centered belief requirements to coercive enforcement across representative Christian settings. Locked score: The 83 rewards excellent relevance, forceful burden contact, and a concrete reply that exposes what a purely lexical account of faith omits, but calibration and evidence remain bounded because the generalization outpaces the supplied comparative documentation.","tags":[]},"con":null}]},{"sectionId":"health-family-and-social-harm","title":"Health, Family, and Social Harm","timebox":"18:22–1:25:36","score":{"pro":79,"con":77},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-medical-deception-and-measles","time":"18:22","role":"Supporting constructive","score":78,"words":"Aron argues that Christian authority spreads preventable medical harm through anti-condom misinformation, anti-vaccination teaching, and advice followed by a measles outbreak.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron provides a concrete behavioral pathway from trusted religious authority through false prevention advice to increased exposure, with condom misinformation, vaccination resistance, and a reported measles outbreak making the public-health risk readily intelligible. Principal limitation: The examples are asserted with limited documentation, combine different Christian actors and health controversies, and do not establish prevalence, countervailing institutional conduct, or whether theology rather than ordinary misinformation produced the outcomes. Live burden: He needs reliable sourcing and comparative evidence showing that Christian authority systematically increases medically dangerous behavior rather than occasionally transmitting errors also found in secular communities. Locked score: The 78 recognizes coherent mechanism, specificity, and strong relevance, while evidential and calibration limits prevent vivid adverse cases from carrying an unsupported general conclusion about Christianity across populations and institutions.","tags":[{"label":"Base-rate neglect","type":"bias","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/base-rate-neglect/","context":"Aron generalizes from anti-condom teaching, anti-vaccination ministry advice, and a resulting measles outbreak."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-meta-analytic-beneficial-pattern","time":"31:23","role":"Major direct reply","score":84,"words":"Jones answers isolated harms with meta-analyses associating intrinsic religiosity with lower crime and substance use, better health, prosociality, and psychological benefits.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones replaces memorable anecdotes with aggregated research, names multiple outcome domains, and explains how meta-analysis identifies broader patterns across varied studies. Principal limitation: Most cited associations concern intrinsic religiosity rather than Christianity specifically; favorable correlation does not establish causation; and study quality, effect magnitude, publication bias, adverse subgroups, and the rapid inventory's details remain largely unexamined. Live burden: He must connect the measured construct to the motion, compare reported benefits with severe minority harms, and show that confounding or selection does not generate the favorable pattern. Locked score: The 84 rewards the debate's strongest positive empirical counterweight, broad evidential coverage, and direct pressure on Aron's generalizations, while reserving higher credit because synthesis breadth alone cannot resolve construct validity or causal attribution.","tags":[{"label":"Scope neglect","type":"bias","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/scope-neglect/","context":"Jones treats average benefits across crime, health, well-being, and prosociality as broadly defeating Christian dangerousness."}]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-prayer-instead-of-treatment","time":"21:35","role":"Load-bearing constructive","score":83,"words":"Aron argues that religious conditioning becomes lethal when parents expect prayer to heal treatable children and therefore deny them effective medicine.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron states an unusually clear causal chain: parents expect supernatural healing, prayer displaces available treatment, and children die from otherwise treatable illness, making the alleged danger direct rather than merely correlational. Principal limitation: The reported hundreds of deaths are undocumented in the exchange, which establishes neither frequency, denominational distribution, legal context, nor whether this neglect represents Christianity broadly instead of an identifiable fringe practice. Live burden: He should substantiate the reported cases and show that authoritative Christian teaching predictably produces treatment refusal despite the many believers and institutions integrating prayer with medicine. Locked score: The 83 reflects powerful motion contact, precision, and a plausible lethal mechanism, while evidential and calibration deductions mark the substantial gap between serious reported cases and a tradition-wide conclusion.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-cross-country-homonegativty-reply","time":"1:24:20","role":"Supporting reply","score":66,"words":"Jones cites cross-national variation and noncausal research on religiosity and homonegativity, but only indirectly addresses Christian parental rejection and youth homelessness.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones correctly resists an automatic causal inference, noting that the relationship between religiosity and homonegative attitudes varies across countries and that the cited research does not itself claim religion causes the outcome. Principal limitation: The reply targets broad cross-country attitudes rather than Aron's specific mechanism of Christian parental rejection producing homelessness among gay and trans youth, and the phrasing about a weaker rise does not establish a decline or a benign effect. Live burden: He needs evidence about family rejection, housing loss, religious motivation, and relevant comparison groups rather than a geographically aggregated attitude study. Locked score: The 66 credits a legitimate causation warning and some relevant counterevidence, but low responsiveness and warrant reflect the mismatch between the study summarized and the concrete family-harm claim requiring an answer.","tags":[{"label":"Red herring","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/red-herring/","context":"Jones answers Christian parental rejection and youth homelessness with cross-country religiosity and homonegativity variation."}]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-youth-homelessness-from-rejection","time":"1:23:53","role":"Supporting constructive","score":75,"words":"Aron links the disproportionate presence of gay and trans youth among homeless young people to rejection by prejudiced Christian parents.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron identifies a vulnerable population, supplies a memorable prevalence range, and proposes an understandable mechanism connecting religiously motivated family rejection to estrangement and loss of housing. Principal limitation: The percentage of homeless youth who are gay or trans does not establish why they became homeless, while the claim that Christian parents caused displacement lacks comparative data, source detail, and attention to other family or economic pathways. Live burden: He must separate minority overrepresentation from causal attribution by documenting parental motives, denominational patterns, counterfactual risks, and the proportion of cases traceable specifically to Christian teaching. Locked score: The 75 rewards substantial relevance and humane specificity, while evidential and calibration scores remain limited because the decisive causal sentence exceeds the evidence presented during the exchange.","tags":[]},"con":null}]},{"sectionId":"cross-national-outcomes-and-climate","title":"Cross-National Outcomes and Climate Policy","timebox":"23:18–1:19:15","score":{"pro":76,"con":87},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-secular-country-outcomes","time":"23:18","role":"Supporting constructive","score":75,"words":"Aron cites cross-country patterns linking lower religiosity with peace, equality, prosperity, rights, and health, while expressly acknowledging China and Vietnam as exceptions.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron presents multiple observable social outcomes rather than relying only on moral intuition, and his explicit acknowledgment of China and Vietnam as exceptions usefully calibrates the claim as a tendency rather than a universal law. Principal limitation: Country-level religiosity is entangled with wealth, governance, education, conflict history, regional culture, and measurement choices, so the unadjusted association cannot identify Christianity as the cause or distinguish individual from national effects. Live burden: He needs multivariable analysis, clear comparison rules, and evidence that the association survives plausible confounders while remaining specific to the motion's Christian target. Locked score: The 75 credits breadth, relevance, and qualified wording, but the central ecological inference—moving from national averages to religious causation—remains insufficiently defended against the strongest live methodological objection.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-cross-country-needs-multivariate-analysis","time":"49:53","role":"Major direct reply","score":88,"words":"Jones argues that national comparisons confound many influences, require multivariable individual analysis, and cannot override evidence that more religious individuals offend less.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones pinpoints the ecological and confounding problems in Aron's national comparison, explicitly demands multivariable individual-level analysis, and offers crime findings that illustrate how national and individual associations can diverge. Principal limitation: The analogy to white-supremacist reasoning is rhetorically distracting, and citing favorable individual correlations does not itself prove causation, represent every social outcome, or establish that intrinsic religiosity maps cleanly onto Christianity. Live burden: He should specify the decisive covariates, explain the individual-level studies' design and effect sizes, and compare like outcomes rather than treating one crime association as a universal rebuttal. Locked score: The 88 reflects exceptional responsiveness, methodological precision, and direct engagement with the inference's weakest bridge, while modest reservations remain about the counterevidence's own causal limits and construct specificity.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-climate-denial-policy","time":"25:12","role":"Supporting constructive","score":76,"words":"Aron uses the Texas Republican platform to argue that the Christian right institutionalizes climate denial, obstructing action on an intergenerational environmental threat.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron identifies a specific policy document and a concrete institutional pathway through which a religiously aligned political bloc could obstruct responses to anthropogenic climate change, thereby connecting belief, power, and foreseeable public harm. Principal limitation: One state party platform cannot establish the position of Christianity generally, and the argument does not disentangle religious commitment from partisan identity, fossil-fuel interests, geography, or broader conservative coalition dynamics. Live burden: He needs evidence that Christian belief independently intensifies climate rejection and materially changes policy after controlling for political ideology and economic interests. Locked score: The 76 rewards a tangible environmental-risk mechanism, recognizable documentary support, and strong relevance, while limited representativeness and causal separation keep the move from demonstrating that Christianity rather than its contingent political alliance drives the dangerous policy.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-climate-acceptance-data","time":"53:16","role":"Supporting reply","score":86,"words":"Jones cites majority Christian acceptance of human-caused climate change and research separating religiosity from conservative political effects on environmental conduct.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones answers the causal claim with directly relevant denominational polling and a study designed to distinguish religiosity from political conservatism, thereby addressing both representativeness and the leading rival explanation. Principal limitation: Majority acceptance does not establish policy influence or behavioral adequacy, denominational categories may conceal politically decisive subgroups, and one reported study cannot settle whether particular theological beliefs affect environmental attitudes under different institutional conditions. Live burden: He should connect acceptance rates to conduct and policy, describe the study's measurements and uncertainty, and test the strongest Christian-right subgroup implicated by Aron's example. Locked score: The 86 rewards precise, source-responsive counterevidence and unusually good confounder awareness, while stopping short of complete rebuttal because broad survey acceptance and null intensification do not exclude concentrated political harm.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-single-study-cannot-counter-meta-analysis","time":"1:18:33","role":"Supporting reply","score":87,"words":"Jones challenges Gregory Paul's univariable country comparison and argues that one methodologically weak study cannot outweigh a synthesis incorporating sixty studies.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones compares evidential designs rather than merely counting citations, identifying univariable visual analysis and distributional problems before asking whether one cross-national paper can overturn a sixty-study meta-analysis. Principal limitation: Study quantity is not automatically evidential quality, the compared research may measure different constructs and levels, and Aron's agreement that one study cannot counter many does not concede that Jones's synthesis answers every harm claim. Live burden: Jones must establish comparability, methodological quality, independence, and causal relevance across the aggregated studies rather than treating the label meta-analysis as dispositive. Locked score: The 87 reflects sharp cross-examination, strong methodological pressure, and a genuinely established narrow concession about comparative evidential weight, while reserving higher credit because neither the synthesis's applicability nor the national study's residual value is fully adjudicated.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"faith-science-and-evidence","title":"Faith, Science, and Evidence","timebox":"20:21–1:14:33","score":{"pro":73,"con":85},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-faith-rejects-scientific-evidence","time":"20:21","role":"Load-bearing constructive","score":69,"words":"Aron defines faith as authority-bound belief resistant to scientific correction, linking supernatural commitments and rejected scientific conclusions to impaired reasoning.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron offers a genuine epistemic mechanism: commitments protected from contrary evidence can prevent correction, and his examples of rejected scientific conclusions make the proposed danger more concrete than a bare definitional dispute. Principal limitation: He treats faith, Christianity, supernatural belief, fallacious reasoning, and science rejection as nearly interchangeable, without establishing that biblical faith requires evidence resistance or that representative Christians cannot hold commitments as defeasible trust. Live burden: He needs a stable definition, authoritative textual support, prevalence evidence, and a demonstrated connection between that form of faith and consequential reasoning failures. Locked score: The 69 preserves credit for relevance, an intelligible correction-failure pathway, and several recognizable examples, but conceptual conflation, thin documentation, and uncharitable generalization weaken logical precision, warrant, and calibration at the argument's central bridge.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-intelligence-correlation-is-fickle","time":"38:00","role":"Supporting reply","score":87,"words":"Jones argues that religion-intelligence correlations weaken, disappear, or reverse across periods and cultures, fitting social conformity better than religious causation.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones engages the research at several levels—its explicit causal limitation, methodological criticism, declining recent effects, and cross-cultural reversal—then offers conformity to local norms as a coherent alternative explanation. Principal limitation: Intelligence correlations are only one indicator of Aron's broader claim about evidence-resistant reasoning, and Jones's compressed survey of criticisms does not provide effect sizes, study quality, or enough detail to compare the conformity hypothesis against other explanations. Live burden: He must show that the qualifications materially undermine the relevant cognitive-harm mechanism, not merely that one psychometric association is context-sensitive and noncausal. Locked score: The 87 rewards excellent responsiveness, calibration, and explanatory competition grounded in research limitations, while the remaining gap reflects incomplete coverage of Aron's wider examples involving evolution, supernatural commitments, and resistance to scientific evidence.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-lacks-greek-expert-concession","time":"1:12:22","role":"Supporting reply","score":85,"words":"Aron candidly concedes that he cannot identify specialist Greek scholars supporting his definition of faith, leaving his linguistic premise evidentially uncertain.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron answers repeated expertise challenge with exceptional candor and precision, explicitly admitting that he doesn't know whether specialist Greek scholars support his definition and refusing to present missionaries as linguistic authorities. Principal limitation: As an argumentative move, this concession provides no affirmative warrant and substantially weakens lexical foundation of his claim that Christian faith necessarily means belief resistant to evidence. Live burden: Aron must produce relevant scholarship, defend his definition through textual usage, or reformulate the epistemic-danger case so it does not depend on contested Greek semantics. Locked score: The 85 rewards truthful responsiveness, clarity, and excellent calibration rather than substantive victory; its relevance component recognizes that this accurate admission advances procedural honesty while leaving the underlying pro burden conspicuously unmet.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-evolution-rejection-not-essential","time":"41:30","role":"Supporting reply","score":86,"words":"Jones uses Christian acceptance rates and premodern nonliteral Genesis readings to argue that evolution rejection is neither universal nor essential to Christianity.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones combines contemporary polling with intellectual history, showing both that many Christians accept evolution and that nonliteral Genesis interpretation predates modern evolutionary theory, which directly challenges claims of necessary incompatibility. Principal limitation: Nonessentiality does not eliminate danger from influential creationist movements, polling categories may conceal differences in scientific understanding, and the historical origin story of young-earth creationism is presented too quickly for independent evaluation. Live burden: He should quantify the relevant rejection, explain its educational or policy consequences, and show why a sizable contingent anti-evolution effect should not count even if Christianity permits alternative readings. Locked score: The 86 rewards strong responsiveness, useful historical counterevidence, and charitable precision, while recognizing that defeating entailment is narrower than disproving recurrent, institutionally consequential science rejection within Christianity.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-faith-means-committed-trust","time":"44:43","role":"Major direct reply","score":85,"words":"Jones appeals to Greek usage and biblical contexts to define faith as evidence-responsive commitment or assurance rather than deliberately blind belief.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones attacks the linguistic premise with specialist Koine Greek usage, Acts, First Peter, and Thomas, arguing that faith means commitment or assurance after evidence, not deliberate blindness. Principal limitation: He compresses scholarly disagreement and generalizes contextually supported meanings into a broad account of Christian faith without fully testing passages or traditions where belief may resist ordinary verification. Live burden: He must show this reading is authoritative and representative enough to displace Aron’s functional examples of evidence resistance, not merely that a rational interpretation is available. Locked score: The 85 rewards excellent responsiveness, clear inferential structure, and substantial textual warrant, but remains below exceptional because lexical range and lived Christian practice are more heterogeneous than he acknowledges.","tags":[]}},{"pro":null,"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-medical-trust-analogy","time":"1:14:12","role":"Supporting reply","score":82,"words":"Jones compares faith beyond sight to trusting a physician's evidence-based diagnosis before symptoms are visible, showing that reliance need not reject evidence.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones's physician analogy cleanly separates immediate personal observation from evidence-mediated trust, demonstrating that walking by faith rather than sight need not semantically entail ignoring evidence or abandoning rational reliance. Principal limitation: Medical trust is anchored in independently testable expertise, institutional safeguards, and diagnostic records, whereas the analogy does not show that supernatural testimony has comparable reliability or correction mechanisms. Live burden: Jones needs to specify which evidence warrants Christian trust and why its quality and accountability make the medical comparison structurally relevant beyond the shared word trust. Locked score: The 82 rewards clarity, responsive conceptual pressure, and a useful counterexample to Aron's simple opposition between faith and evidence, while evidential warrant remains moderated because the analogy establishes compatibility with rational trust more successfully than Christianity's actual evidential credentials.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"causation-benefits-and-interpretation","title":"Causation, Benefits, and Interpretive Responsibility","timebox":"27:48–1:27:22","score":{"pro":67,"con":80},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-belief-benefit-is-placebo","time":"56:12","role":"Supporting reply","score":62,"words":"Aron argues that subjective benefits may be placebo-like and coexist with harmful conduct, so favorable associations do not vindicate specific doctrines.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron identifies a legitimate relevance gap: subjective well-being can coexist with false belief or harmful behavior, and broad beneficial associations do not automatically answer dangers attributed to particular doctrines. Principal limitation: Calling the findings placebo effects is unsupported, the response does not engage Jones's crime, substance-use, health, and prosocial outcomes as more than feelings, and listing doctrines does not demonstrate their harmful consequences. Live burden: Aron needs evidence that the measured benefits are merely interpretive artifacts and separate causal studies connecting scapegoating, original sin, or flood belief to dangerous conduct. Locked score: The 62 retains credit for distinguishing truth from benefit and general outcomes from doctrine-specific harms, but weak warrant, partial responsiveness, and dismissive characterization leave the meta-analytic evidence substantially unanswered rather than successfully rebutted.","tags":[{"label":"Red herring","type":"fallacy","url":"https://logfall.com/fallacies/red-herring/","context":"Aron recasts objective and subjective beneficial findings as feelings, then pivots to Christianity’s truth and specific doctrines."}]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-attribution-and-necessary-cause","time":"27:48","role":"Major direct reply","score":81,"words":"Jones formalizes the missing causal steps between Christian identity and harmful action, arguing that examples and associations alone cannot establish doctrinal responsibility.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones reconstructs Aron's inference into distinct steps and repeatedly separates actor identity, association, shared interpretation, and doctrinal causation, giving the con case a clear standard that applies across several opening examples. Principal limitation: The demand sometimes approaches necessary or intrinsic causation when the ordinary motion could be satisfied by Christianity materially increasing risk, and the atheism-autism analogy illustrates attribution error without matching Aron's proposed textual and institutional mechanisms. Live burden: Jones must state an appropriately comparative causal threshold and address evidence of contribution, enablement, or predictable amplification rather than requiring Christianity to be the sole indispensable cause. Locked score: The 81 rewards coherence, broad responsiveness, and disciplined burden analysis, with reservations for potentially narrowing the motion and for treating heterogeneous harm claims through one generalized causal objection.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-placebo-and-personal-experience-reply","time":"1:20:59","role":"Supporting reply","score":56,"words":"Aron recasts reported benefits as subjective interpretation, preserves his compelled-belief concern, and declines to accept an unfamiliar study during cross-examination.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron reasonably refuses to endorse an unfamiliar paper on demand and distinguishes feeling benefited from having truth-sensitive or rationally corrective beliefs, preserving a conceptually relevant epistemic concern. Principal limitation: Personal placebo experience cannot characterize the populations in Jones's studies, subjective interpretation does not explain objective outcomes such as crime or substance use, and refusing immediate acceptance is not evidence against the cited synthesis. Live burden: Aron needs a researched methodological critique or counterevidence showing that expectancy, selection, reporting bias, or compelled belief explains the reported benefits and produces net danger. Locked score: The 56 recognizes a defensible caution about unseen research and a relevant distinction between well-being and rationality, but low warrant, limited engagement, and anecdotal substitution leave the major empirical challenge essentially unresolved.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-specific-beliefs-require-studies","time":"57:01","role":"Supporting reply","score":81,"words":"Jones argues that doctrines sounding objectionable do not establish danger unless evidence connects those specific beliefs to measurable harmful effects.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones returns the exchange to a disciplined evidential question, separating moral or intuitive reactions to a doctrine from empirical proof that holding it causes dangerous outcomes. Principal limitation: He understates conceptual and historical evidence, since doctrines can authorize conduct or structure institutions without modern population studies, while demanding a separate study for every belief risks an unrealistically narrow standard. Live burden: He should specify what combination of textual, historical, comparative, and statistical evidence establishes doctrine-specific risk while distinguishing causal contribution from interpretive possibility. Locked score: The 81 rewards concise responsiveness, logical clarity, and a proper demand for a defended doctrine-to-harm bridge, but is tempered because he asserts this methodological requirement more readily than he justifies it across different kinds of alleged danger.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-interpretive-variability-enables-atrocity","time":"1:25:32","role":"Major direct reply","score":78,"words":"Aron argues that recurring incompatible interpretations and atrocities justified as divine commands make Christianity foreseeably dangerous even if defenders call each misuse mistaken.","critique":"Strongest feature: Aron answers the misuse defense by shifting from authorial endorsement to foreseeable system effects: recurring incompatible interpretations make communicative ambiguity relevant to responsibility. Principal limitation: His broad claims about atrocities and salvation by belief establish neither the frequency nor comparative rate of harmful readings, and risk making any repeatedly misused text causally responsible without a limiting principle. Live burden: Aron must identify which features of Christian scripture or institutions predictably amplify dangerous readings, then distinguish that influence from deliberate exploitation that would occur with unrelated language. Locked score: The 78 rewards motion-level synthesis, direct responsiveness, and a serious account of interpretive risk, while evidence, precision, and calibration remain constrained because foreseeability and recurring misuse are asserted more forcefully than comparatively demonstrated.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-words-do-not-cause-deliberate-misuse","time":"1:26:56","role":"Major direct reply","score":77,"words":"Jones argues by analogy that deliberate violent misuse of someone's words does not establish that the speaker or underlying message caused persecution.","critique":"Strongest feature: Jones offers an intuitive limiting case for source responsibility, asking whether violent exploitation of Aron's own words would make Aron causally responsible, and correctly distinguishes advocacy from another person's deliberate distortion. Principal limitation: The analogy does not address degrees of ambiguity, authority, repetition, institutional reinforcement, or foreseeability, and Aron only denies advocating violence; he never concedes Jones's broader causal principle. Live burden: Jones must explain why biblical texts and Christian interpretive institutions resemble arbitrary distortion of Aron's speech rather than a system whose content predictably licenses competing harmful applications. Locked score: The 77 rewards clear responsive pressure and a useful distinction between message and misuse, but the incomplete analogy and absence of the purported causal concession leave Aron's interpretive-responsibility thesis challenged rather than defeated.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":75,"strengths":["Connected abstract dangerousness to concrete mechanisms, including coercive law, treatment refusal, family rejection, climate obstruction, and interpretations used to justify atrocities.","Used specific scriptural commands and institutional examples, then repeatedly returned to harms suffered by identifiable people rather than debating theology alone.","Made useful concessions and qualifications, excluding political imperialism, acknowledging national exceptions, and admitting uncertainty about specialist Greek support."],"blunders":[{"text":"Relied on vivid cases and unadjusted national comparisons without adequately establishing prevalence, denominators, confounders, or Christianity-specific causal contribution.","links":[{"label":"Base-rate neglect","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/base-rate-neglect/"}]},{"text":"Overstated disputed generalizations about religious rule, faith, placebo effects, and parental causation beyond the documentation supplied in the assessed rounds.","links":[]},{"text":"Left Jones's aggregated beneficial evidence largely unanswered, substituting doctrinal examples, personal experience, and reasonable caution about an unseen study for comparative analysis.","links":[{"label":"Confirmation bias","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/confirmation-bias/"}]}]},"con":{"score":82,"strengths":["Consistently separated Christian identity, textual misuse, association, and causal responsibility, giving the con case a stable answer across heterogeneous harms.","Brought aggregated studies, methodological criticism, historical interpretation, polling, and alternative explanations into direct contact with Aron's central evidence.","Won the cross-national and faith-science exchanges through precise analysis of confounders, careful covenantal distinctions, and evidence-responsive accounts of how trust is justified."],"blunders":[{"text":"Sometimes narrowed the motion from dangerousness to intrinsic or necessary causation, risking an excessively demanding standard that overlooks probabilistic contribution and enablement.","links":[]},{"text":"Treated favorable religiosity meta-analyses as broadly dispositive without fully addressing construct validity, causal direction, heterogeneous subgroups, or severe minority harms.","links":[{"label":"Scope neglect","url":"https://cogbias.site/biases/scope-neglect/"}]},{"text":"Used imperfect analogies for white-supremacist comparisons and misused speech, and the final analogy did not secure Aron's concession on causal responsibility.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"Christianity can be publicly dangerous when authoritative texts, institutions, and identity commitments predictably increase serious harms even without being their sole cause.","premises":["A fair danger standard asks whether Christianity materially raises the probability or severity of harm, not whether it is a necessary cause.","Some authoritative biblical passages portray coercion against religious dissent as divinely commanded, creating interpretive resources unavailable to an entirely secular justification.","Christian institutions and families sometimes translate religious authority into medical refusal, suppression of dissent, minority rejection, or resistance to corrective evidence.","Recurring harmful interpretations remain relevant when a tradition preserves the texts, authority structures, and salvation stakes that make those interpretations predictable.","Beneficial average associations cannot erase concentrated catastrophic harms; evaluation must compare severity, distribution, counterfactual frequency, and preventability together."],"conclusion":"Christianity is dangerous to the extent that its distinctive authoritative mechanisms produce a measurable excess risk of grave, foreseeable, and preventable harm.","notice":"This AI-generated contribution is hypothetical and not a quotation or an argument made by either speaker; it does not affect the debate scores."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Risk amplification under concentrated authority","text":"Danger can arise from interaction rather than a doctrine acting alone. When religious certainty combines with parental control, political office, medical authority, or weak legal safeguards, ordinary mistakes become harder to correct because dissent acquires spiritual costs. The relevant comparison is therefore not believers versus nonbelievers in the abstract, but otherwise similar institutions with different levels of sacred authorization and external accountability. Evidence could test whether harmful decisions persist longer, resist contrary expertise more strongly, or spread faster when leaders present them as divine obligations. A demonstrated amplification effect would answer Jones's causal challenge without claiming that Christianity is necessary or sufficient for every resulting harm."},{"title":"Asymmetric tail-risk assessment","text":"Average well-being or crime benefits may coexist with rare but devastating outcomes such as fatal treatment refusal, organized persecution, or durable family expulsion. A responsible danger assessment should therefore examine the entire distribution of consequences rather than only the mean association reported in broad meta-analyses. The empirical question becomes whether Christian practices create identifiable high-severity tails, which groups bear them, and whether those losses are compensated by benefits enjoyed elsewhere. This framework avoids dismissing Jones's favorable evidence while showing why it cannot by itself resolve the motion. Prevalence, severity, reversibility, subgroup concentration, and plausible counterfactuals would all become explicit parts of the comparison."},{"title":"Correction-cost mechanism","text":"Beliefs differ not only in whether they begin with evidence but in how costly revision becomes. A Christian commitment can be surrounded by family belonging, eternal stakes, leadership legitimacy, and moral identity, making disconfirmation socially and psychologically expensive even for a believer who originally had reasons. That mechanism is narrower and more testable than defining every instance of faith as blind belief. Research could compare response to failed predictions, medical guidance, or historical correction among matched participants whose beliefs carry different sacred and communal costs. If Christian commitments systematically impose higher correction costs in consequential domains, the result would support an epistemic-danger claim without relying on intelligence correlations or disputed Greek semantics."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"Christianity is not shown dangerous merely by Christian involvement in harm; the motion requires comparative evidence that distinctively Christian commitments increase net risk.","premises":["Actor identity, textual availability, and correlation are insufficient when political incentives, culture, poverty, or institutional weakness can independently explain the alleged harm.","Ancient covenantal commands are not automatically contemporary Christian prescriptions, and recurring misuse must be distinguished from representative doctrinal application.","Aggregated research on intrinsic religiosity reports favorable associations across several behavioral and health outcomes that isolated adverse examples cannot simply displace.","Cross-national comparisons require multivariable analysis because country averages combine political history, prosperity, education, regional culture, and individual religiosity.","Christianity permits evidence-responsive faith, evolutionary acceptance, medical trust, and climate-science acceptance, defeating claims that anti-scientific conduct is doctrinally necessary."],"conclusion":"Until the pro side demonstrates a Christianity-specific increase in serious net harm against credible alternatives, the broad claim of dangerousness remains unproved.","notice":"This AI-generated contribution is hypothetical and not a quotation or an argument made by either speaker; it does not affect the debate scores."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Matched-institution comparison","text":"The cleanest causal test would compare Christian and non-Christian institutions operating under similar wealth, law, political competition, education, and professional oversight. Schools, hospitals, charities, and families could be matched on these background conditions, then evaluated for measurable harms and benefits using preregistered outcomes. This design would improve on both Aron's country averages and Jones's broad religiosity syntheses by bringing the explanatory target closer to Christianity itself. It would also reveal whether harmful effects cluster in particular governance structures or doctrines rather than across the tradition. Without such matched comparisons, both spectacular abuses and favorable population correlations remain vulnerable to selection, confounding, and category mismatch."},{"title":"Internal correction capacity","text":"A tradition's danger should depend partly on whether it contains effective resources for correcting its own abuses. Christianity includes covenantal distinctions, commands of neighbor-love, traditions of natural reason, denominational accountability, and institutions that accept medicine and scientific revision. These resources do not erase atrocities, but they can show that harmful interpretations are contestable from within rather than straightforward outputs of the religion. The empirical extension would measure whether internal critics successfully change teaching, remove abusive leaders, cooperate with civil oversight, and reduce repeated harm. Demonstrated correction capacity would weaken the inference from historical misuse to intrinsic danger while avoiding the implausible claim that Christian people or organizations never cause harm."},{"title":"Specified net-risk threshold","text":"The motion needs a decision rule that treats benefit and harm symmetrically. Before labeling Christianity dangerous, evaluators should specify which outcomes matter, how severity is weighted, what comparison population is appropriate, and what increase above baseline counts as material. The same rule must credit lower crime or substance misuse if reliable while counting medical neglect, coercion, or rejection at their full seriousness. This prevents favorable averages from hiding vulnerable minorities and prevents exceptional tragedies from defining hundreds of millions of heterogeneous adherents. A transparent net-risk threshold would turn the debate from dueling examples into a falsifiable comparative assessment and clarify exactly what evidence could change either side's conclusion."}]}},"year":2019,"assessmentScopeDisclosure":"Only the opening statements and cross-examination (1:59–1:27:22) are assessed. All subsequent audience questions and answers, and both speakers' later closing statements, are excluded; neither speaker receives credit or penalties for that material. These scores are not an assessment of the complete recording."};
